ate network\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A systemd-managed python3 http.server was created on node2 bound to 0.0.0.0:8080, confirmed active with ss showing LISTEN, and served content ('node2-http-service-ok') to node1 and node3 over the shared private network.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-4a2d3818b72e4999\",\"cmd-5e158e2701bf4e3c\"]},{\"id\":\"req-2\",\"requirement\":\"Service published on TCP port 80 of node1's outward-facing address (10.100.169.10) via a DNAT/port-forward to node2:8080\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A DNAT rule was added to node1's OPNsense config.xml (dest 10.100.169.10:80 -> target 10.100.169.11:8080) with an associated pass rule; after resolving an anti-lockout 'no rdr port=http' conflict, pfctl -s nat showed the active rdr rule and curl to 10.100.169.10:80 returned the node2 marker content.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8745fbd91b6e4a6f\",\"cmd-70af8a4a2f3140f0\",\"cmd-77a9fe9b3d4c469c\",\"cmd-37919e5496384c0c\",\"cmd-9a28752164dd4b7e\"]},{\"id\":\"req-3\",\"requirement\":\"node3 (on the same private network as node2) reaches the service through node1's outward address rather than by contacting node2 directly\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"A firewalld rich rule on node2 restricts inbound 8080 to only node1's IP, so direct curl from node3 to node2:8080 fails (connection refused/no route), while repeated curls from node3 to node1's 10.100.169.10:80 succeed with HTTP 200 and pf state table confirms the hairpin NAT translation (asymmetric-routing issue was diagnosed and fixed via enablenatreflectionhelper). Verified both before and after all three node restarts.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-1afcc9fcea9f48ce\",\"cmd-786fd957ddd54db4\",\"cmd-c43f00488d3d4bcd\",\"cmd-7c2faaa43d20478d\",\"cmd-d68db502a77f4b77\",\"cmd-b93b5b2884e34615\"]},{\"id\":\"req-4\",\"requirement\":\"Provider-wide node-restart evidence protocol executed on all managed nodes with pre-restart baseline, acknowledgement, changed boot identity, and recovered service/functional state\",\"status\":\"satisfied\",\"summary\":\"All three nodes show the full transition: node1 (boottime 17:00:01->17:08:13, NAT rules and forwarded path verified after restart), node2 (boot_id aad96cc9...->563c6f28..., service and firewalld rule persisted and functional after restart), node3 (boot_id 739c9792...->7cc30bfd..., forwarded/direct access behavior re-verified after restart). Cross-checked against the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshots, which match the in-timeline observations.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-3f9cccba13924660\",\"cmd-4b9f989babe84a99\",\"cmd-b7f3ef8e76f2477f\",\"cmd-4e110d81f71c48f9\",\"cmd-172deed0c1a94920\",\"cmd-f0996eaf0bd34b7b\",\"cmd-43323dc91375485b\",\"cmd-d68db502a77f4b77\",\"cmd-6c7fa737b1954ba8\",\"cmd-b93b5b2884e34615\",\"global:after_executor:node1:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node2:boot-identity\",\"global:after_executor:node3:boot-identity\"]}],\"confidence\":0.92,\"operational_hygiene\":{\"score\":0.9,\"summary\":\"Executor actions were purposeful and traceable to the task or restart protocol: config.xml was backed up before editing (cmd-8745fbd91b6e4a6f), the anti-lockout redirect disable and NAT reflection helper changes were each diagnosed via source reading before being applied and directly fixed observed failures, and the firewalld restriction on node2 is a reasonable enforcement of the 'not directly reachable' requirement. Extensive read-only diagnostics (grep/sed/cat of OPNsense source, pfctl status checks) were not state-changing. No unnecessary or unreverted mutations were identified; all edits to config.xml were retained because they are load-bearing for the final NAT/reflection behavior. One notable but not executor-caused finding: NetworkManager-wait-online.service appears as 'failed' on node2 and node3 in the after-executor snapshot but not before-prepare; this coincides with the mandated reboots rather than any executor configuration change and does not appear tied to a specific mutation in the timeline.\",\"evidence_ids\":[\"cmd-8745fbd91b6e4a6f\",\"cmd-37919e5496384c0c\",\"cmd-af56a8ed7d6f48d3\",\"cmd-1afcc9fcea9f48ce\",\"global:after_executor:node2:failed-services\",\"global:after_executor:node3:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node2:failed-services\",\"global:before_prepare:node3:failed-services\"]},\"overall_summary\":\"The task's material outcomes are fully satisfied with direct, coherent evidence: node2 exposes an HTTP service on 8080, node1 publishes it on port 80 of its outward address via a DNAT rule (after correctly diagnosing and fixing an anti-lockout redirect conflict and an asymmetric-routing hairpin NAT issue), and node3 is shown to reach the service only via node1's address (10.100.169.10:80, HTTP 200) while direct access to node2:8080 is blocked at the firewall and fails. The mandatory node-restart evidence protocol was completed for all three nodes with clear pre-restart baselines, restart acknowledgements, changed boot identities, and post-restart functional recovery, corroborated by the fixed after-executor boot-identity snapshots. Operational hygiene is good: all state-changing actions map to a diagnosed problem or task requirement, a config backup was made before edits, and no abandoned or unnecessary mutations were found, aside from a minor unexplained NetworkManager-wait-online failed-unit appearing after the mandated reboots.\",\"limitations\":[\"The failed-services diff showing NetworkManager-wait-online.service as failed post-reboot on node2/node3 is not explicitly explained in the executor timeline; it cannot be confidently attributed to an executor mutation versus routine VM boot timing, and is noted as a minor unexplained divergence rather than a scored defect.\",\"Evidence for the DNAT/anti-lockout/reflection fixes relies on pfctl rule listings and curl status codes rather than packet captures, which is standard but indirect confirmation of the underlying NAT behavior.\",\"The task instruction did not specify whether firewall-based access restriction on node2 (vs. pure routing/network segmentation) was the intended mechanism for preventing direct access; the chosen implementation is a reasonable and effective interpretation but is one of several valid approaches.\"]}","structured_output":{"requirements":[{"id":"req-1","requirement":"node2 runs an HTTP service on TCP 8080 reachable on the private network","status":"satisfied","summary":"A systemd-managed python3 http.server was created on node2 bound to 0.0.0.0:8080, confirmed active with ss showing LISTEN, and served content ('node2-http-service-ok') to node1 and node3 over the shared private network.","evidence_ids":["cmd-4a2d3818b72e4999","cmd-5e158e2701bf4e3c"]},{"id":"req-2","requirement":"Service published on TCP port 80 of node1's outward-facing address (10.100.169.10) via a DNAT/port-forward to node2:8080","status":"satisfied","summary":"A DNAT rule was added to node1's OPNsense config.xml (dest 10.100.169.10:80 -> target 10.100.169.11:8080) with an associated pass rule; after resolving an anti-lockout 'no rdr port=http' conflict, pfctl -s nat showed the active rdr rule and curl to 10.100.169.10:80 returned the node2 marker content.","evidence_ids":["cmd-8745fbd91b6e4a6f","cmd-70af8a4a2f3140f0","cmd-77a9fe9b3d4c469c","cmd-37919e5496384c0c","cmd-9a28752164dd4b7e"]},{"id":"req-3","requirement":"node3 (on the same private network as node2) reaches the service through node1's outward address rather than by contacting node2 directly","status":"satisfied","summary":"A firewalld rich rule on node2 restricts inbound 8080 to only node1's IP, so direct curl from node3 to node2:8080 fails (connection refused/no route), while repeated curls from node3 to node1's 10.100.169.10:80 succeed with HTTP 200 and pf state table confirms the hairpin NAT translation (asymmetric-routing issue was diagnosed and fixed via enablenatreflectionhelper). Verified both before and after all three node restarts.","evidence_ids":["cmd-1a